# verify your users:
docker exec ldap ldapsearch `
  -x `
  -D "cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com" `
  -w adminpassword `
  -b "dc=example,dc=com" `
  "(objectClass=inetOrgPerson)" `
  cn mail

# modify one of the users:
docker exec ldap ldapmodify `
  -x `
  -H "ldap://127.0.0.1:389" `
  -D "cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com" `
  -w adminpassword `
  -f /container/service/slapd/assets/config/bootstrap/ldif/custom/modify-carol.ldif

After you’ve filled in the basic connection fields (Vendor, Connection URL, Bind DN, Bind Credential, Users DN), scroll down to the Advanced Settings section and set the following:
• Edit mode
– READ_ONLY
• Keycloak will only read users from LDAP.
– WRITABLE (optional)
• If you want Keycloak to create or update LDAP entries directly, choose WRITABLE.
• Users DN*
– ou=Users,dc=example,dc=com
• This is the full DN under which your user entries live.
• Relative user creation DN
– (leave blank)
• Leaving this empty means any new users Keycloak creates go directly under your Users DN.
• To isolate new users in a sub-OU, you could enter e.g. ou=NewUsers.
• Username LDAP attribute*
– uid
• The attribute Keycloak uses as the login name (e.g. uid=alice,…).
• RDN LDAP attribute*
– uid
• The attribute used to construct each entry’s RDN. Matches your cn=… or uid=… naming scheme.
• UUID LDAP attribute*
– entryUUID
• OpenLDAP’s built-in unique entry identifier. You can verify it by running:
      docker exec ldap ldapsearch -x -LLL -b dc=example,dc=com \
        "(uid=alice)" entryUUID
      ```
• User object classes*
– inetOrgPerson, organizationalPerson, person, top
• A comma-separated list of objectClasses to use when Keycloak creates new entries.
• inetOrgPerson is required; including its parent classes ensures compatibility.
After you save these settings do a Test connection, Test authentication, then Save and Sync all users. Now Keycloak knows exactly where (and how) to look for your LDAP users—and, if you chose WRITABLE, where to create them too.